{%- comment -%}
  Cashback attribution — who counts as a partner shopper, decided once per
  visit, in the head.

  Usage: {% render 'cashback-attribution' %} from layout/theme.liquid, before
  anything paints.

  ── Why this is JavaScript and why it is in the head ─────────────────────

  Attribution arrives as a query parameter on the landing URL, and Liquid
  cannot read one. It also has to outlive that landing page: the partner
  sends a shopper to a product, and the thing that has to change is the cart
  two pages later. So the parameter is read once, kept on the device for the
  partner's own attribution window, and republished on every later page as a
  class on <html>.

  It is in the head, inline and synchronous, because the class gates whether
  a shopper sees a discount-code box. Deferred, the box would paint, then
  vanish — which is both an invitation to go and look for a code and a
  flicker on every page of the session.

  ── What the class is for ────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Exclusivity. A partner pays the shopper a percentage of an order we have
  also discounted ourselves, and we pay for both. Theme settings can switch
  our own promotions off wholesale, but a store that later runs a weekend
  code should not thereby break an exclusivity commitment. So the lock is
  independent of that switch: `cb-on` hides every promotional surface from a
  partner-referred shopper whatever the rest of the site is doing.

  ── What this cannot do ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Stop a determined shopper. Clearing site data, a different browser or a
  private window drops the lock, and /discount/CODE still applies a code
  that exists. The only enforcement that actually holds is in admin: if a
  code is not supposed to be live, delete it, do not merely hide it. This is
  the honest-shopper layer, and it is the layer that decides what almost
  everyone sees.

  ── The cart attribute ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The partner will send an invoice listing the orders they claim. The only
  way to check it is to have recorded, on our side, which orders arrived
  through them — so the click id is written onto the cart as an attribute
  and travels into the order in admin. It also makes double-payment visible:
  an order tagged both `cashback_source` and a paid-ads click is one we are
  being billed for twice.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign cb_on = false
  if settings.cashback_enabled and settings.cashback_exclusive
    assign cb_on = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if cb_on -%}
  <script>
    (function () {
      /* Lower-cased here so the comparison below does not have to care how
         the sources were typed into theme settings. */
      var SOURCES = {{ settings.cashback_sources | default: '' | downcase | split: ',' | json }}
        .map(function (s) { return s.trim(); })
        .filter(Boolean);
      var DAYS = {{ settings.cashback_window_days | default: 30 | times: 1 | json }};
      var KEY = 'tfs_cashback';

      if (!SOURCES.length) return;

      var q;
      try { q = new URLSearchParams(location.search); } catch (e) { return; }

      /* utm_source is the conventional one, but partners are inconsistent and
         some send only ?ref=. All three are checked rather than asking the
         partner to change their links, which is the request that never gets
         actioned. */
      var src = '';
      ['utm_source', 'ref', 'source'].forEach(function (p) {
        if (src) return;
        var v = (q.get(p) || '').trim().toLowerCase();
        if (SOURCES.indexOf(v) > -1) src = v;
      });

      var stored = null;
      try { stored = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || 'null'); } catch (e) {}

      if (src) {
        /* A fresh click overwrites an older one: last click wins, which is
           what every cashback platform's own attribution does. The click id
           is whatever the partner appends — kept verbatim, because it is
           their reference and ours is useless to them in a dispute. */
        stored = {
          src: src,
          cid: (q.get('click_id') || q.get('clickid') || q.get('cid') || '').slice(0, 128),
          at: Date.now()
        };
        try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(stored)); } catch (e) {}
      } else if (stored && stored.at && (Date.now() - stored.at) > DAYS * 864e5) {
        /* Past the window the partner no longer credits the order, so neither
           may we: leaving the lock on would suppress our own offers in
           exchange for a cashback that is never paid. */
        try { localStorage.removeItem(KEY); } catch (e) {}
        stored = null;
      }

      if (!stored || !stored.src) return;

      document.documentElement.classList.add('cb-on');
      window.CASHBACK = stored;

      /* Written once per landing, not on every page: /cart/update.js is a
         real request, and the attribute only has to be on the cart by the
         time there is an order. Deferred to load so it never competes with
         the first paint it was placed in the head to protect. */
      if (!src) return;
      addEventListener('load', function () {
        fetch('{{ routes.cart_url }}/update.js', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify({
            attributes: {
              'cashback_source': stored.src,
              'cashback_click_id': stored.cid || '',
              'cashback_clicked_at': new Date(stored.at).toISOString()
            }
          })
        }).catch(function () { /* attribution is not worth a broken cart */ });
      });
    })();
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── Where the partner's own tracking goes ────────────────────────────────

  Not here, in all likelihood. Everything above is ours: it decides what the
  storefront shows and records the click id on the cart so an invoice can be
  checked. The partner's own conversion tracking is a separate thing, and
  where it belongs depends entirely on what they issue:

    · A Shopify app — install it in admin and add nothing here. This is the
      usual case and the one to push for; it is the only route that sees the
      real order rather than a guess at it.

    · A checkout or thank-you pixel — Settings › Customer events in admin,
      as a custom pixel. It cannot go in a theme file: Shopify does not
      render checkout from this theme, so a tag placed here never fires on
      the page that matters and will silently report nothing.

    · A server-to-server postback on the order webhook — a private app or a
      function, again outside the theme.

  A storefront tag, if they insist on one, is the only variant that belongs
  in this file, and it goes directly below. Nothing is placed here on a guess
  — an attribution tag that fires on the wrong page is worse than none, since
  it reports numbers that look plausible and are not.
{%- endcomment -%}
